<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- No PackageReference at all, deliberately - and this is the third time that sentence is written in this
       repo, with the hardest argument behind it. DigitalOcean has no first-party .NET SDK, so hand-rolling was
       the only maintained option. Azure has one and it was declined over its dependency closure and
       Azure.Identity's ambient credential discovery. AWS has one too (AWSSDK.EC2 + AWSSDK.Core), and declining
       it costs something the other two did not: AWS authentication is not a header, it is a signing algorithm.

       The argument for declining it anyway:

         * AWSSDK.Core carries its own credential-resolution chain (environment variables, the shared
           credentials file, EC2/ECS instance metadata, SSO caches). That is the same objection raised against
           Azure.Identity and it is the stronger one here, because instance-metadata resolution succeeds
           silently on any EC2 host. Servyx's rule is that credentials come from ISecretStore by URN and from
           nowhere else; a library whose default is to go looking is the opposite of that rule.
         * AWSSDK.Core's credential type is `AWSCredentials`, which materialises the secret access key as a
           `string` property. Servyx's SecretLease exists precisely so a secret is a zeroable byte[] and never
           an interned, unerasable string. Using the SDK would mean giving that up at the one place it matters.
         * The surface actually needed is five EC2 Query actions and one signing algorithm.

       So SigV4 is implemented here from AWS's published specification over System.Security.Cryptography's
       HMACSHA256 and SHA256, and the EC2 Query API over System.Net.Http + System.Xml.Linq - all of which ship
       in the net10.0 shared framework, so "no new dependency" here is a fact about the build, not an
       aspiration. The signer is validated against AWS's own published SigV4 test-suite vectors; see
       AwsSigV4Tests, which pins the exact expected signatures.

       Note the sibling infra projects reference Microsoft.Extensions.{DependencyInjection,Logging}.Abstractions
       for their DI registration helpers; this project has neither, which is also why it can carry no logger and
       therefore no code path that could log the AWS secret access key, a derived signing key, or a signature. -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Depends on Servyx.Domain directly, not Servyx.Application, for the same reason
         Servyx.Infrastructure.Docker, .Ssh, .DigitalOcean and .Azure do: Application's purpose is to consume
         IProvisioner/ITransport via DI, so a reference the other way would create a cycle. It also references
         no other infrastructure project - in particular not Servyx.Infrastructure.Ssh, even though every
         TargetDescriptor it produces is consumed by the SSH transport, and neither of the two existing cloud
         adapters, even though this is the third instance of the same shape. That is the layering rule and it is
         load-bearing here: if this project could see the SSH adapter, "shape I contains no install logic" would
         be a matter of discipline rather than a matter of what is reachable. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Core\Servyx.Domain\Servyx.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
